# Every interface object whose `new X()` / `X()` pair this engine does not yet answer Chrome 152's
# way, one `<realm> <interface>` per line, sorted. Written by `tests/ctor_messages_live.rs`, which
# compares a live window realm and a live dedicated Worker against
# `fixtures/chrome-152-ctor-messages.json` -- a recording of Chrome 152.0.7977.82, headed on the
# Wayland desktop, over a loopback page, 2026-10-16. Goal invariant I5: a sorted manifest, never a count.
#
# A LINE MAY ONLY LEAVE BY BEING FIXED. The gate fails if an unpinned name differs (a regression) AND
# if a pinned name now agrees (progress that must be recorded), so this file cannot drift in either
# direction. `KORVUS_UPDATE_CTOR_GAPS=1` rewrites it, keeping this header.
#
# THE BARE-CALL CLASS IS CLOSED (SSOT W7.3, 34 rows: 21 window + 13 worker). Every remaining row
# below differs in its `new X()` answer only -- the bare `X()` half of all 27 is Chrome's, except the
# five the two seams named at the end of this header still own. W7.1's note here said the class was
# "19 window + 13 worker hand bodies, one missing line each" and pointed at
# `install::live::rows::build_newable`; re-measured on a live release build it was 26 window + 15
# worker and NOT one mechanism but three, only one of which build_newable could have reached:
#
#   * a hand `cb_*_ctor` body with no `new.target` check (`Highlight`, `Path2D`, `CSSStyleSheet`,
#     `AudioContext`, `OfflineAudioContext`, `AudioBuffer`, and the shared body behind
#     `AudioProcessingEvent`/`OfflineAudioCompletionEvent`). Not "returns undefined": `Highlight()`
#     re-prototyped the WINDOW onto `Highlight.prototype` and `Path2D()` stamped a path id on it.
#   * the hand-table `Model::Value` PLACEHOLDER in `installer::create_interface_in`, which was a no-op
#     body and so returned `undefined` on a call (`ClipboardItem`, `CustomElementRegistry`,
#     `DOMParser`, `XMLSerializer`, `ReadableStreamDefaultReader`, `WritableStreamDefaultWriter`), and
#     the same shape hand-written in `install::live::bootstrap::mint_event_target` for the worker's
#     `EventTarget`.
#   * a `deno_core` cppgc CLASS TEMPLATE standing in for the interface object. `deno_ops` generates a
#     `#[constructor]` that ends in `cppgc::wrap_object(scope, args.this(), …)` and never reads
#     `new.target`, so `DOMRect()`, `DOMPoint()`, `DOMQuad()`, `DOMMatrix()`, `WebKitCSSMatrix()`,
#     `Headers()`, `FormData()`, `TextEncoder()` and `TextDecoder()` WRAPPED THE GLOBAL OBJECT as the
#     instance and handed it back -- `[object Window]` in the page, `[object DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope]`
#     in a worker. `install::live::rows::guard_native_class_ctors` gives each of them a Rust interface
#     object over the same class prototype; op2 has no `new.target` hook to fix it at the class.
#
#   * a hand body throws a DIFFERENT wrong message on `new` (`AudioBuffer`, `KeyframeEffect`,
#     `IntersectionObserver`, `PerformanceObserver`, `ResizeObserver`, `OfflineAudioContext`,
#     `VTTCue`, `ClipboardItem`, `ReadableStreamDefaultReader`, `WritableStreamDefaultWriter`):
#     Chrome's is the WebIDL argument count and this engine's is its own validation, or it mints where
#     Chrome throws. The bare-call half of every one of them is Chrome's since W7.3.
#   * an interface the registry row does NOT own because another layer replaced it: `AbstractRange`,
#     `StaticRange` and `EventTarget` are JS classes ("Class constructor X cannot be invoked without
#     'new'"), and `Screen()` returns the Window. That is the `host.js` seam (W4.j), not the ctor
#     builders' -- the window `EventTarget` is `host.js`'s class; the WORKER's was this unit's and is
#     fixed.
#   * `CSSMathSum`/`Min`/`Max`/`Product`: Chrome runs a real body that throws "Arguments can't be
#     empty"; this engine has no body, so its shell constructor mints. Honest for a shell.
#   * `SpeechGrammar`, `TimelineTrigger`, `WebSocketError`: Chrome constructs them with no arguments and
#     this engine answers the illegal pair. `_declares_ctor` cannot promote them from the capture
#     (`length: 0`, `newable: true`) without promoting `Array` and `Object` with them, because webref
#     declares a constructor for none of them. (`SpeechGrammarList` left this list with REALWEB H35: webref DOES declare
#     its `constructor()`, which the generator's IDL scan missed while it read speech-api.idl's prose
#     "This interface has been deprecated" as `interface has`.)
#   * `WebKitMutationObserver`: Chrome's messages name `MutationObserver`, because the two globals are
#     ONE function object there and two interface objects here. An aliasing defect, not a message one.
#
# EMPTY SINCE 2026-10-11 (challenge batch 81): the last nine rows left by the argument count coming first --
# AudioBuffer, IntersectionObserver, PerformanceObserver (both realms), ResizeObserver, VTTCue (its required count
# in the constructor's data), OfflineAudioContext and KeyframeEffect (overload resolution by count: two arguments
# "Overload resolution failed.", one the copy constructor) -- and WebKitMutationObserver published as MutationObserver
# itself. Any line below is a regression.

